1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,585
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,585 --> 00:00:04,857
Episode 2.

3
00:00:04,857 --> 00:00:08,011
Pip and the Upside-Down Library.

4
00:00:08,011 --> 00:00:11,036
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:11,036 --> 00:00:14,602
Why was sorting only by color a problem?

6
00:00:14,602 --> 00:00:16,193
Ready?

7
00:00:16,193 --> 00:00:18,394
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,394 --> 00:00:21,349
The school library needs help.

9
00:00:21,349 --> 00:00:25,696
Books are on tables, chairs, and even a windowsill.

10
00:00:25,696 --> 00:00:27,940
Pip is excited.

11
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:30,710
I can sort them, he says.

12
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:34,758
When Maya returns, every shelf looks beautiful.

13
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:37,372
Red books are together.

14
00:00:37,372 --> 00:00:39,857
Blue books are together.

15
00:00:39,857 --> 00:00:43,551
One green book sits beside a green plant.

16
00:00:43,551 --> 00:00:46,406
Where is the book about sharks?

17
00:00:46,406 --> 00:00:48,423
she asks.

18
00:00:48,423 --> 00:00:50,482
What color is it?

19
00:00:50,482 --> 00:00:52,542
Pip replies.

20
00:00:52,542 --> 00:00:54,985
I do not remember.

21
00:00:54,985 --> 00:00:57,741
Then we have a small problem.

22
00:00:57,741 --> 00:01:01,392
Leo wants a story by his favorite author.

23
00:01:01,392 --> 00:01:05,568
Pip asks whether the cover is dark blue or light blue.

24
00:01:05,568 --> 00:01:08,394
Leo does not know that either.

25
00:01:08,394 --> 00:01:11,875
Ms. Park brings three books to the table.

26
00:01:11,875 --> 00:01:14,318
One is about whales.

27
00:01:14,318 --> 00:01:16,789
One is about the Moon.

28
00:01:16,789 --> 00:01:20,582
One is a story about a whale visiting the Moon.

29
00:01:20,582 --> 00:01:25,724
They all have blue covers, Maya says, but their subjects are different.

30
00:01:25,724 --> 00:01:29,062
The librarian explains their system.

31
00:01:29,062 --> 00:01:31,960
Stories are in one section.

32
00:01:31,960 --> 00:01:35,482
Books that explain real things are in another.

33
00:01:35,482 --> 00:01:39,559
Signs and labels help readers find smaller groups.

34
00:01:39,559 --> 00:01:42,869
Let me help you, Leo says to Pip.

35
00:01:42,869 --> 00:01:46,548
They check the labels together and move the books.

36
00:01:46,548 --> 00:01:49,943
The shark book joins the animal books.

37
00:01:49,943 --> 00:01:53,110
The Moon book joins the space books.

38
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:55,610
Where do I put the plant?

39
00:01:55,610 --> 00:01:57,670
Pip asks.

40
00:01:57,670 --> 00:02:01,008
By the window, says the librarian.

41
00:02:01,008 --> 00:02:04,417
Pip adds a little sign: Not a book.

42
00:02:04,417 --> 00:02:07,826
Please do not return it through the book slot.

43
00:02:07,826 --> 00:02:10,980
Now, a little more to discover.

44
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:15,127
Sorting means putting things into groups using a rule.

45
00:02:15,127 --> 00:02:21,065
You can sort the same objects in different ways: by size, color, or purpose.

46
00:02:21,065 --> 00:02:25,170
A useful system depends on what you need to find.

47
00:02:25,170 --> 00:02:31,505
Libraries use organized systems and labels so that other people can follow the rules, too.

48
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:34,673
Let us hear the main idea again.

49
00:02:34,673 --> 00:02:37,457
Pip sorted books by color.

50
00:02:37,457 --> 00:02:43,196
The shelves looked nice, but readers could not easily find a subject or an author.

51
00:02:43,196 --> 00:02:47,159
The children followed the library's labels instead.

52
00:02:47,159 --> 00:02:50,738
A good sorting rule helps people do a job.

53
00:02:50,738 --> 00:02:53,877
Here are three useful expressions.

54
00:02:53,877 --> 00:02:57,059
You can listen, or say them with me.

55
00:02:57,059 --> 00:02:59,218
They need our help.

56
00:02:59,218 --> 00:03:01,377
They need our help.

57
00:03:01,377 --> 00:03:04,019
Let me help you sort them.

58
00:03:04,019 --> 00:03:06,661
Let me help you sort them.

59
00:03:06,661 --> 00:03:09,076
What rule are we using?

60
00:03:09,076 --> 00:03:11,491
What rule are we using?

61
00:03:11,491 --> 00:03:14,488
Remember our listening question?

62
00:03:14,488 --> 00:03:18,053
Why was sorting only by color a problem?

63
00:03:18,053 --> 00:03:20,340
Here is the answer.

64
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:26,619
Readers often knew a subject or an author, but they did not remember the color of the cover.

65
00:03:26,619 --> 00:03:30,582
And here is a question just for your imagination.

66
00:03:30,582 --> 00:03:35,795
How would you organize a box of toys so that a friend could find one quickly?

67
00:03:35,795 --> 00:03:39,105
There is more than one possible answer.

68
00:03:39,105 --> 00:03:42,400
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
